Package: saat
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: sudo-megas <gonzalorodriguez761@gmail.com>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.35), libegl1, libgl1, libwayland-client0, libwayland-cursor0, libwayland-egl1, libxcb1, libxcb-cursor0, libxcb-icccm4, libxcb-image0, libxcb-keysyms1, libxcb-render-util0, libxcb-shape0, libxcb-util1, libxcb-xkb1, libxkbcommon-x11-0
Homepage: https://github.com/sudo-megas/SAAT
Description: catalogue a mechanical-watch collection
 SAAT is a local, offline desktop application for cataloguing a personal
 wristwatch collection. It organises watches into a photo-forward grid, a
 dense spec table and a wear calendar, shows a detail page per watch,
 compares watches side by side, and records what was worn on which day.
 .
 It ships empty. Every watch is entered by its owner -- there is no seed
 data, no demo collection and no online catalogue to import from.
 .
 There is no database and no network access. Each watch is a plain,
 editable TOML file on disk beside its own images, under
 ~/.local/share/saat, so a collection stays readable with a text editor
 and survives this program being removed. Removing or purging this
 package never deletes one.
 .
 This package bundles its own Qt 6 and Python runtime instead of using
 the system's. That makes it large -- roughly 230 MB installed, almost
 all of it Qt -- and it is the honest cost of the trade: the binary is
 the same one the portable tarball ships, tested as one build, rather
 than a second build against whichever PySide6 version a given Debian or
 Ubuntu release happens to carry.
